{"id":"n-20261016-goz1ak","category":"tech","display_title":"World Timekeepers Adopt Leap Hour","display_title_he":"שומרי הזמן בעולם מאמצים את שעת העיבור","short_summary":"International timekeepers have voted to replace the problematic leap second with a leap hour to protect computer systems from technical failures.","english_level":"C1","words":513,"reading_time":4,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/oct/15/times-up-for-the-leap-second-as-scientists-swap-it-for-the-leap-hour","title":"How an accelerating Earth led experts to adopt the leap hour","published_at":"2026-10-15T16:16:10.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-15T16:16:10.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-16T05:57:26.670Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["International delegates gathered at a conference in France to vote on a major change to global timekeeping, deciding to replace the tiresome leap second with a new system known as the leap hour. Representatives from sixty-nine countries cast their votes at the International Bureau of Weights and Measures, an organization that ensures the world adheres to consistent standards of time, distance, and weight. The decision, set to take effect from next year, aims to mitigate the severe risks that computers and technical infrastructure face when software has to grapple with minutes lasting sixty-one seconds or, even worse, fifty-nine seconds instead of a standard round sixty.","Dr Setnam Shemar, a principal scientist in the time and frequency department at the UK’s National Physical Laboratory and a member of the expert committee that proposed the measure, noted that after thirty years of debate, the leap second is finally making way for the leap hour. This transition will reduce hazards to national infrastructure and digital systems that rely on accurate timekeeping, while still preserving the fundamental link between global time and the rotation of the Earth. The vote held in Versailles means that coordinated universal time will now be permitted to drift by up to an hour from astronomical time before an actual leap hour becomes necessary, though experts estimate that such an event is unlikely to happen for thousands of years.","While the more familiar leap year was introduced over two millennia ago to keep the calendar aligned with Earth's orbit around the sun, leap seconds are a modern invention born from the creation of the world's first practical, high-precision caesium atomic clock in 1955 by Louis Essen and Jack Parry. Since 1967, the formal definition of a second has been based on atomic vibrations rather than the Earth's rotation, which is far less reliable due to influences from the planet's molten core, ocean shifts, and global jet streams. Although Earth's rotation has broadly slowed down over long epochs—meaning a day during the age of the dinosaurs lasted about twenty-three and a half hours—officials have nonetheless added twenty-seven leap seconds since 1972 to align coordinated universal time with astronomical time.","However, in the past decade, the planet's spin unexpectedly sped up, raising a thirty percent probability of requiring the first-ever negative leap second in the near future. While positive leap seconds have previously caused servers to freeze and websites such as Reddit and LinkedIn to crash—and even delayed Qantas flights—negative leap seconds presented an unprecedented threat. Because companies would have to spend a fortune testing all interconnected global computer systems for a scenario never encountered before, the international committee swung the vote in favor of the leap hour. Historically, the UK grumbled about abandoning the leap second due to Greenwich mean time, and Russia opposed the shift over concerns regarding its Glonass satellite navigation system. Dr Christian Bizouard, director of the Paris-based IERS Earth Orientation Centre, which tracks Earth's movements and issues leap second notices, approved of the vote while observing that it ultimately pushes the decision onto people living in the distant future."],"facts":[{"fact":"Delegates from 69 countries voted at a conference in France of the International Bureau of Weights and Measures to replace the leap second with the leap hour.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The change aims to reduce risks to computer systems and national infrastructure from irregular minutes.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Earth's rotation has recently sped up, raising concerns about potential negative leap seconds which carry severe software risks.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"A leap hour is unlikely to be needed for thousands of years.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"international","lemma":"international","pos":"adj","he":"בינלאומי","name":false},{"text":"delegates","lemma":"delegate","pos":"n","he":"צירים / נציגים","name":false},{"text":"gathered","lemma":"gather","pos":"v","he":"התאספו","name":false},{"text":"conference","lemma":"conference","pos":"n","he":"ועידה","name":false},{"text":"France","lemma":"France","pos":"n","he":"צרפת","name":"true"},{"text":"vote","lemma":"vote","pos":"v","he":"להצביע","name":false},{"text":"major","lemma":"major","pos":"adj","he":"עיקרי / מרכזי","name":false},{"text":"change","lemma":"change","pos":"n","he":"שינוי","name":false},{"text":"global","lemma":"global","pos":"adj","he":"עולמי","name":false},{"text":"timekeeping","lemma":"timekeeping","pos":"n","he":"מדידת זמן","name":false},{"text":"deciding","lemma":"decide","pos":"v","he":"להחליט","name":false},{"text":"replace","lemma":"replace","pos":"v","he":"להחליף","name":false},{"text":"tiresome","lemma":"tiresome","pos":"adj","he":"מעייף / טורדני","name":false},{"text":"leap second","lemma":"leap second","pos":"n","he":"שנייה מעוברת","name":false},{"text":"system","lemma":"system","pos":"n","he":"מערכת","name":false},{"text":"leap hour","lemma":"leap hour","pos":"n","h